-- NEGATIVE fixture for the future D-RECURSIVE check (spec §6.4.2, §6.3.9).
--
-- p2, PARAMETERISED arm. Two top-level DECIDEs that call each other:
--
--     `even step` n  reads  `odd step`
--     `odd step`  n  reads  `even step`
--
-- Under Phase 5 both become BUSINESS KNOWLEDGE MODELS, so the cycle lives on
-- the knowledgeRequirement graph and is §6.3.9's arm. The nullary arm of the
-- same shape is a DIFFERENT graph and a different check, which is why it has
-- its own file: cycle-p2-mutual-nullary.l4. Writing p2 once would have merged
-- two findings into one fixture.
--
-- WHY AN OWN-NAME-MENTION DETECTOR MISSES IT: neither body mentions its own
-- name. Each mentions the OTHER's. Only an SCC over the requirement graph sees
-- this, which is §6.4.2's conclusion and the reason both p2 files exist.
--
-- On today's tree the emitted DRG IS cyclic here and the fidelity report never
-- says so.
--
-- Expected after Phase 5: one D-RECURSIVE over an SCC of size 2, listing the
-- name AND id of both members, range = Nothing.
--
--
-- PROVENANCE OF THIS HEADER. The .l4 source was typechecked (`l4 check`, clean)
-- when it was written on 2026-07-31, with a prebuilt CLI. The statements above
-- about what TODAY'S EXPORTER emits and reports are taken from spec
-- §6.4.2/§6.4.5's table and were NOT re-measured here: that session had no
-- build. Whoever wires these fixtures into jl4/tests/DmnExport.hs must MEASURE
-- the expectation rather than transcribe it.
-- NEVER added to `goldenSubjects`.

IMPORT prelude

§ `mutual recursion, parameterised`

GIVEN n IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`even step` n MEANS
    IF n EQUALS 0 THEN TRUE ELSE `odd step` (n MINUS 1)

GIVEN n IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`odd step` n MEANS
    IF n EQUALS 0 THEN FALSE ELSE `even step` (n MINUS 1)
